import LocalAlgebrasLean

/-!
# The core statements of LocalAlgebrasLean

This file states, without proofs, the main theorems of LocalAlgebrasLean on nets of local
algebras and on the quasi-local algebra of a lattice of quantum systems with finite alphabets:
nets over regions with an orthogonality relation, their local commutativity and covariance, and
the nets generated by sets; the qudit net of a finite set of sites, whose algebras consist of the
matrices local to a set of sites, with alphabets that may depend on the site, its local
commutativity and its covariance under relabelling of the sites, for one alphabet at every
site; the local embeddings of the
matrix algebras of finite sets of sites into the bounded operators on the Hilbert space of the
finitely supported configurations, the net of local algebras with local commutativity and
isotony, its covariance under symmetries of the sites, its independence of the reference
configuration, the universal property of the quasi-local algebra, and the case of finitely many
sites. A final section gives examples.

The statements use eighteen definitions of the library, and every other notion in them is
Mathlib's. Each of the eighteen is fixed in Mathlib's terms by a theorem below: `RegionIndex`, its
isomorphisms `RegionIndex.Iso` (through their maps of regions) and `subsetIndex` by
`region_index_iff`; `Net` (the algebra of a region `O` is written `𝒜 O`) and `Net.generated` by
`net_iff`; `Net.IsGenerating`, `Net.IsLocallyCommutative` and `Net.IsCovariancePair` by
`net_properties_iff`; `Config`, `QuditAlgebra`, `embed` and `quditNet` by `qudit_net`; `relabel`
by `relabel_covariance` (a star isomorphism, determined by its values); `FinConfig` by
`finConfig_def`, `ConfigSpace` by `configSpace_def`, `localEmbed` by `inner_localEmbed` (its
matrix elements in the standard basis of `ℓ²`, which determine a bounded operator),
`quasiLocalNet` by `quasiLocalNet_eq_closure` (the algebra of a set of sites `O` is written
`quasiLocalNet s₀ O`), and `quasiLocalAlgebra` by `quasiLocalAlgebra_def`.

Every statement here is closed by `sorry`. The file `Solution.lean` in this folder repeats each
statement with the identical text and proves it. The tool `lake comparator`, configured by
`comparator.json` in this folder, checks that each theorem named there has exactly the same
statement in both files and that its proof in `Solution.lean` uses no axioms other than
`propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`. The script `check_same_statements.py` checks the
same correspondence on the text of the two files.

Conventions. The norm on matrices is the operator norm for the `ℓ²` norm
(`open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator` where it is used). Where a reference configuration `s₀`
is given, every alphabet is nonempty, because `s₀` picks a letter at each site. A finite set of
sites is a `Finset Λ` or a set `X : Set Λ` with an instance `Fintype X`; a statement about such a
set holds for every choice of the instance. Covariance under a group acting on the sites is stated
for one alphabet at every site and a reference configuration invariant under the group.
-/

open LocalAlgebrasLean LocalAlgebrasLean.Qudit

namespace Sealed

/-! ## Nets

Let $R$ be a preordered set of regions and $A$ a C$^*$-algebra. A star subalgebra is a unital
complex star subalgebra, and a star isomorphism is a unital complex $*$-isomorphism. A region index
on $R$ consists of an orthogonality relation $O_1 \perp O_2$ on the regions. -/

/-- Region indices and their isomorphisms. A relation $r$ on $R$ is the orthogonality relation of
a region index exactly when it is symmetric and antitone in its first argument ($O_0 \le O_1$ and
$r(O_1, O_2)$ give $r(O_0, O_2)$), and a region index is determined by its relation. For region
indices $I$ on $R$ and $I'$ on a preordered set $R'$, with orthogonality relations $\perp$ and
$\perp'$, an isomorphism from $I$ to $I'$ has a map $R \to R'$; a map $f$ is the map of an
isomorphism exactly when it is the map of an order isomorphism and $f(O_1) \perp' f(O_2) \iff
O_1 \perp O_2$ for all regions $O_1, O_2$, and an isomorphism is determined by its map. For a set
$V$ of sites, the region index of the subsets of $V$, ordered by inclusion, has two subsets
orthogonal exactly when they are disjoint. -/
theorem region_index_iff {R R' : Type*} [Preorder R] [Preorder R'] (r : R → R → Prop)
    (I : RegionIndex R) (I' : RegionIndex R') (f : R → R') (V : Type*) :
    ((∃ J : RegionIndex R, J.Orthogonal = r) ↔
      (∀ O₁ O₂, r O₁ O₂ → r O₂ O₁) ∧ ∀ O₀ O₁ O₂, O₀ ≤ O₁ → r O₁ O₂ → r O₀ O₂) ∧
    (∀ J : RegionIndex R, J.Orthogonal = I.Orthogonal → J = I) ∧
    ((∃ κ : I.Iso I', ⇑κ = f) ↔ (∃ e : R ≃o R', ⇑e = f) ∧
      ∀ O₁ O₂, I'.Orthogonal (f O₁) (f O₂) ↔ I.Orthogonal O₁ O₂) ∧
    (∀ κ κ' : I.Iso I', ⇑κ = ⇑κ' → κ = κ') ∧
    ∀ O₁ O₂ : Set V, (subsetIndex V).Orthogonal O₁ O₂ ↔ Disjoint O₁ O₂ := sorry

/-- Nets and generated nets. A net over $R$ in $A$ assigns to each region $O$ a star subalgebra
$\mathcal A(O)$ of $A$; an assignment $O \mapsto F(O)$ of star subalgebras is a net exactly when
it is monotone ($O \le O'$ implies $F(O) \subseteq F(O')$, isotony) and every $F(O)$ is
norm-closed, and a net is determined by its algebras. For a monotone family $O \mapsto G(O)$ of
subsets of $A$, the generated net assigns to each region $O$ the norm closure of the star
subalgebra of $A$ generated by $G(O)$ (a unital one, so that it contains the unit, and is
$\mathbb C 1$ when $G(O)$ is empty). -/
theorem net_iff {R : Type*} [Preorder R] {A : Type*} [CStarAlgebra A]
    (F : R → StarSubalgebra ℂ A) (G : R →o Set A) :
    ((∃ 𝒜 : Net R A, ∀ O, 𝒜 O = F O) ↔ Monotone F ∧ ∀ O, IsClosed (F O : Set A)) ∧
    (∀ 𝒜 𝒜' : Net R A, (∀ O, 𝒜 O = 𝒜' O) → 𝒜 = 𝒜') ∧
    ∀ O, Net.generated G O = (StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (G O)).topologicalClosure := sorry

/-- Properties of nets. Let $\mathcal A$ be a net over $R$ in $A$ and $I$ a region index on $R$
with orthogonality $\perp$. Generation: $\mathcal A$ is generating exactly when the norm closure of
the star subalgebra generated by all its algebras is $A$. Local commutativity: $\mathcal A$ is
locally commutative for $I$ exactly when, whenever $O_1 \perp O_2$, every element of
$\mathcal A(O_1)$ commutes with every element of $\mathcal A(O_2)$. Covariance: let $\mathcal A'$
be a net over a preordered set $R'$ in a C$^*$-algebra $A'$, $I'$ a region index on $R'$,
$\kappa$ an isomorphism from $I$ to $I'$ and $\alpha : A \to A'$ a star isomorphism; then
$(\kappa, \alpha)$ is a covariance pair of $\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal A'$ exactly when
$\alpha(\mathcal A(O)) = \mathcal A'(\kappa(O))$ for every region $O$. -/
theorem net_properties_iff {R R' : Type*} [Preorder R] [Preorder R'] {A A' : Type*}
    [CStarAlgebra A] [CStarAlgebra A'] (I : RegionIndex R) (I' : RegionIndex R')
    (𝒜 : Net R A) (𝒜' : Net R' A') (κ : I.Iso I') (α : A ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ] A') :
    (𝒜.IsGenerating ↔ (⨆ O, 𝒜 O).topologicalClosure = ⊤) ∧
    (Net.IsLocallyCommutative I 𝒜 ↔
      ∀ O₁ O₂, I.Orthogonal O₁ O₂ → ∀ a ∈ 𝒜 O₁, ∀ b ∈ 𝒜 O₂, Commute a b) ∧
    (Net.IsCovariancePair 𝒜 𝒜' κ α ↔ ∀ O, (𝒜 O).map α.toStarAlgHom = 𝒜' (κ O)) := sorry

/-- Nets generated by sets. Let $O \mapsto G(O)$ be a monotone family of subsets of $A$ over $R$,
with generated net $\mathcal A$. If the norm closure of the star subalgebra generated by the union
of all $G(O)$ is $A$, then $\mathcal A$ is generating. Let $I$ be a region index on $R$ with
orthogonality $\perp$: if every $G(O)$ is closed under the adjoint and every element of $G(O_1)$
commutes with every element of $G(O_2)$ whenever $O_1 \perp O_2$, then $\mathcal A$ is locally
commutative for $I$. Let $I'$ be a region index on a preordered set $R'$, $A'$ a C$^*$-algebra,
$O \mapsto G'(O)$ a monotone family of subsets of $A'$ over $R'$ with generated net
$\mathcal A'$, $\kappa$ an isomorphism from $I$ to $I'$ and $\alpha : A \to A'$ a star
isomorphism with $\alpha(G(O)) = G'(\kappa(O))$ for every region $O$: then $(\kappa, \alpha)$ is a
covariance pair of $\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal A'$. -/
theorem generated_net_properties {R R' : Type*} [Preorder R] [Preorder R'] {A A' : Type*}
    [CStarAlgebra A] [CStarAlgebra A'] (I : RegionIndex R) (I' : RegionIndex R')
    (G : R →o Set A) :
    ((StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (⋃ O, G O)).topologicalClosure = ⊤ →
      (Net.generated G).IsGenerating) ∧
    ((∀ O, ∀ a ∈ G O, star a ∈ G O) →
      (∀ O₁ O₂, I.Orthogonal O₁ O₂ → ∀ a ∈ G O₁, ∀ b ∈ G O₂, Commute a b) →
      Net.IsLocallyCommutative I (Net.generated G)) ∧
    ∀ (G' : R' →o Set A') (κ : I.Iso I') (α : A ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ] A'), (∀ O, α '' G O = G' (κ O)) →
      Net.IsCovariancePair (Net.generated G) (Net.generated G') κ α := sorry

/-! ## The qudit net

Let $\Lambda$ be a finite set of sites, each site $u$ carrying a finite alphabet $\sigma_u$; the
alphabets may differ from site to site. A configuration $s$ chooses a letter $s(u) \in \sigma_u$
at every site. The qudit algebra is the algebra of complex matrices indexed by the
configurations, with the matrix product and the conjugate transpose, a C$^*$-algebra with the
operator norm of the matrix acting on the Euclidean space
of complex functions on the configurations. For a set of sites $X$, a matrix on the letters of $X$
is a complex matrix indexed by the assignments of a letter in $\sigma_x$ to each $x \in X$, and
$s|_X$ is the restriction of a configuration $s$ to $X$. -/

open Classical in
/-- The qudit algebra, the embedding and the qudit net. A configuration is a function assigning
to each site $u$ a letter in $\sigma_u$, and the qudit algebra consists of the complex matrices
indexed by the configurations. For a set of sites $X$ and a matrix $m$ on the letters of $X$, the
embedding $j_X(m)$ is the matrix with $j_X(m)_{s s'} = m_{s|_X, s'|_X}$ if the configurations $s$
and $s'$ agree at every site outside $X$, and $0$ otherwise. The qudit net is a net over the sets
of sites in the qudit algebra; a matrix $M$ lies in its algebra $\mathcal A(X)$ of a set of sites
$X$ exactly when (1) $M_{s s'} = 0$ whenever $s$ and $s'$ differ at some site outside $X$, and
(2) $M_{s s'} = M_{r r'}$ whenever $s$, $s'$ agree outside $X$, $r$, $r'$ agree outside $X$,
$s = r$ on $X$ and $s' = r'$ on $X$. -/
theorem qudit_net {Λ : Type*} [Fintype Λ] [DecidableEq Λ] (σ : Λ → Type*)
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] :
    Config σ = ((u : Λ) → σ u) ∧
    QuditAlgebra σ = Matrix ((u : Λ) → σ u) ((u : Λ) → σ u) ℂ ∧
    (∀ (X : Set Λ) (m : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ) (s s' : (u : Λ) → σ u),
      embed X m s s' = if ∀ u ∉ X, s u = s' u then m (fun x => s x) (fun x => s' x) else 0) ∧
    ∀ (X : Set Λ) (M : Matrix ((u : Λ) → σ u) ((u : Λ) → σ u) ℂ), M ∈ quditNet σ X ↔
      (∀ s s' : (u : Λ) → σ u, ¬ (∀ u ∉ X, s u = s' u) → M s s' = 0) ∧
      ∀ s s' r r' : (u : Λ) → σ u, (∀ u ∉ X, s u = s' u) → (∀ u ∉ X, r u = r' u) →
        (∀ u ∈ X, s u = r u) → (∀ u ∈ X, s' u = r' u) → M s s' = M r r' := sorry

open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator in
/-- If every alphabet is nonempty, then for every set of sites $X$ the algebra $\mathcal A(X)$ of
the qudit net consists exactly of the matrices $j_X(m)$ with $m$ a matrix on the letters of $X$.
The algebra $\mathcal A(\Lambda)$ of the set of all sites is the whole qudit algebra. Local
commutativity: the qudit net is locally commutative for the region index of the subsets of
$\Lambda$, in which two sets of sites are orthogonal exactly when they are disjoint. -/
theorem qudit_net_range_and_locality {Λ : Type*} [Fintype Λ] [DecidableEq Λ] (σ : Λ → Type*)
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] :
    ((∀ u, Nonempty (σ u)) → ∀ (X : Set Λ) (M : Matrix ((u : Λ) → σ u) ((u : Λ) → σ u) ℂ),
      M ∈ quditNet σ X ↔ ∃ m, embed X m = M) ∧
    (∀ M : Matrix ((u : Λ) → σ u) ((u : Λ) → σ u) ℂ, M ∈ quditNet σ Set.univ) ∧
    Net.IsLocallyCommutative (subsetIndex Λ) (quditNet σ) := sorry

/-- Relabelling covariance. Let $\Lambda$ and $\Lambda'$ be finite sets of sites with one finite
alphabet $Q$ at every site, and $e : \Lambda \to \Lambda'$ a bijection. The relabelling by $e$ is
a star isomorphism $\rho_e$ from the qudit algebra of $\Lambda$ onto the qudit algebra of
$\Lambda'$ with $(\rho_e M)_{t t'} = M_{t \circ e, t' \circ e}$ for every matrix $M$ in the qudit
algebra of $\Lambda$ and all configurations $t, t'$ of $\Lambda'$; and
$\rho_e(\mathcal A(X)) = \mathcal A'(e(X))$ for every set of sites $X \subseteq \Lambda$, where
$\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal A'$ are the qudit nets of $\Lambda$ and $\Lambda'$. -/
theorem relabel_covariance {Λ Λ' Q : Type*} [Fintype Λ] [DecidableEq Λ] [Fintype Λ']
    [DecidableEq Λ'] [Fintype Q] [DecidableEq Q] (e : Λ ≃ Λ') :
    (∀ (M : Matrix (Λ → Q) (Λ → Q) ℂ) (t t' : Λ' → Q),
      relabel e M t t' = M (t ∘ e) (t' ∘ e)) ∧
    ∀ X : Set Λ, (quditNet (fun _ : Λ => Q) X).map (relabel e).toStarAlgHom =
      quditNet (fun _ : Λ' => Q) (e '' X) := sorry

/-! ## Setting

There are sites $\Lambda$ (any set, possibly infinite), a finite alphabet $\sigma_u$ at every
site $u$, and a reference
configuration $s_0$ choosing a letter at every site (so every alphabet is nonempty). For a finite
set of sites $X$, a matrix on the letters of $X$ is a complex matrix indexed by the assignments
$\xi$ of a letter $\xi_x \in \sigma_x$ to each $x \in X$, that is, an operator on
$\bigotimes_{x \in X} \mathbb C^{\sigma_x}$. Bounded operators carry the operator norm, and
C$^*$-algebras are complex. -/

/-- The finitely supported configurations are the configurations $s$ that differ from the
reference configuration $s_0$ at finitely many sites. -/
theorem finConfig_def {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) :
    FinConfig s₀ = {s : (u : Λ) → σ u // {u | s u ≠ s₀ u}.Finite} := sorry

/-- The Hilbert space $H$ is $\ell^2(F)$, the square-summable complex functions on the set $F$ of
finitely supported configurations. -/
theorem configSpace_def {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) :
    ConfigSpace s₀ = lp (fun _ : FinConfig s₀ => ℂ) 2 := sorry

/-! ## The local embeddings

For a finite set of sites $X$, the local embedding $\iota_X$ is a unital $*$-homomorphism from the
matrices on the letters of $X$; this is part of the type of $\iota_X$ in the Lean statements.
Statement 10 determines $\iota_X$ by its matrix elements. -/

open Classical in
/-- For a finite set of sites $X$, a matrix $m$ on the letters of $X$ and finitely supported
configurations $s, t$, the matrix element $\langle e_s, \iota_X(m) e_t\rangle$ in the standard
basis of $\ell^2(F)$ is $m(s|_X, t|_X)$ if $s$ and $t$ agree at every site outside $X$, and $0$
otherwise. -/
theorem inner_localEmbed {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) (X : Set Λ) [Fintype X]
    (m : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ) (s t : FinConfig s₀) :
    inner ℂ (lp.single 2 s (1 : ℂ)) (localEmbed s₀ X m (lp.single 2 t 1)) =
      if ∀ u ∉ X, s.1 u = t.1 u then m (fun x : X => s.1 x) (fun x : X => t.1 x) else 0 := sorry

open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator in
/-- For a finite set of sites $X$, the local embedding $\iota_X$ is injective and
$\|\iota_X(m)\| = \|m\|$ for every matrix $m$ on the letters of $X$, the norm of $m$ being its
operator norm on $\ell^2$. -/
theorem localEmbed_injective_and_norm {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) (X : Set Λ)
    [Fintype X] :
    Function.Injective (localEmbed s₀ X) ∧
      ∀ m : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ, ‖localEmbed s₀ X m‖ = ‖m‖ := sorry

open Classical in
/-- Compatibility: for finite sets of sites $X \subseteq Y$ and a matrix $m$ on the letters of
$X$, $\iota_Y(m \otimes 1) = \iota_X(m)$, where $(m \otimes 1)(\zeta, \zeta') = m(\zeta|_X,
\zeta'|_X)$ if the letters $\zeta, \zeta'$ on $Y$ agree outside $X$, and $0$ otherwise. -/
theorem localEmbed_extend {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {X Y : Set Λ} [Fintype X] [Fintype Y]
    (hXY : X ⊆ Y) (m : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ) :
    localEmbed s₀ Y (Matrix.of fun ζ ζ' : (y : Y) → σ y =>
      if ∀ y : Y, (y : Λ) ∉ X → ζ y = ζ' y then
        m (fun x => ζ ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) (fun x => ζ' ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) else 0) =
      localEmbed s₀ X m := sorry

/-- Local commutativity: for disjoint finite sets of sites $X$ and $Y$, $\iota_X(a)$ and
$\iota_Y(b)$ commute
for all matrices $a$ on the letters of $X$ and $b$ on the letters of $Y$. -/
theorem commute_localEmbed_of_disjoint {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {X Y : Set Λ}
    [Fintype X] [Fintype Y] (hXY : Disjoint X Y) (a : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ)
    (b : Matrix ((y : Y) → σ y) ((y : Y) → σ y) ℂ) :
    Commute (localEmbed s₀ X a) (localEmbed s₀ Y b) := sorry

/-! ## The net of local algebras

Every set of sites $O$ has a $*$-subalgebra $\mathcal A(O)$ of the bounded operators on $H$, closed
in norm (statement 14), and the quasi-local algebra is $\mathcal A_{ql} = \mathcal A(\Lambda)$. -/

/-- For every set of sites $O$, the algebra $\mathcal A(O)$ is the norm closure, in the bounded
operators on $H$, of the set of operators $\iota_X(m)$ with $X \subseteq O$ a finite set of sites
and $m$ a matrix on the letters of $X$. -/
theorem quasiLocalNet_eq_closure {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) (O : Set Λ) :
    (quasiLocalNet s₀ O : Set (ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀)) =
      closure {a | ∃ X : Finset Λ, (X : Set Λ) ⊆ O ∧
        ∃ m : Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ,
          localEmbed s₀ (X : Set Λ) m = a} := sorry

/-- The quasi-local algebra $\mathcal A_{ql}$ is the algebra $\mathcal A(\Lambda)$ of the set of
all sites. -/
theorem quasiLocalAlgebra_def {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) :
    quasiLocalAlgebra s₀ = quasiLocalNet s₀ Set.univ := sorry

/-- Local commutativity: for disjoint sets of sites $O_1$ and $O_2$, every element of $\mathcal
A(O_1)$
commutes with every element of $\mathcal A(O_2)$. -/
theorem commute_of_mem_quasiLocalNet {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {O₁ O₂ : Set Λ}
    (h : Disjoint O₁ O₂) {a b : ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀}
    (ha : a ∈ quasiLocalNet s₀ O₁) (hb : b ∈ quasiLocalNet s₀ O₂) : Commute a b := sorry

/-- Isotony: for sets of sites $O_1 \subseteq O_2$, $\mathcal A(O_1) \subseteq \mathcal A(O_2)$. -/
theorem quasiLocalNet_mono {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {O₁ O₂ : Set Λ} (h : O₁ ⊆ O₂) :
    quasiLocalNet s₀ O₁ ≤ quasiLocalNet s₀ O₂ := sorry

/-! ## Symmetries and the reference configuration -/

/-- Covariance: let a group $\Gamma$ act on the sites, with one finite alphabet $Q$ at every site
and a reference configuration $s_0$ with $s_0(gu) = s_0(u)$. For each $g \in \Gamma$ there is a
unitary $U$ on $H$ with $U \iota_X(m) U^* = \iota_{gX}(g_* m)$ for every finite set of sites $X$,
where $gX = \{y : g^{-1} y \in X\}$ and $(g_* m)(\zeta, \zeta') = m(\zeta \circ g, \zeta' \circ g)$,
and with $U \mathcal A(O) U^* = \mathcal A(gO)$ for every set of sites $O$. The unitary is asserted
for each $g$ separately; the statement does not say that $g \mapsto U$ is a representation. -/
theorem exists_unitary_covariance {Γ Λ Q : Type*} [Group Γ] [MulAction Γ Λ] [DecidableEq Λ]
    [Fintype Q] [DecidableEq Q] (s₀ : Λ → Q) (hs₀ : ∀ (g : Γ) (u : Λ), s₀ (g • u) = s₀ u)
    (g : Γ) :
    ∃ U ∈ unitary (ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀),
      (∀ (X : Set Λ) [Fintype X] [Fintype ({y | g⁻¹ • y ∈ X} : Set Λ)]
        (m : Matrix ((x : X) → Q) ((x : X) → Q) ℂ),
        U * localEmbed s₀ X m * star U =
          localEmbed s₀ ({y | g⁻¹ • y ∈ X} : Set Λ)
            (Matrix.of fun ζ ζ' : (y : ({y | g⁻¹ • y ∈ X} : Set Λ)) → Q =>
              m (fun x => ζ ⟨g • x, by simp⟩) (fun x => ζ' ⟨g • x, by simp⟩))) ∧
      ∀ O : Set Λ, (fun a => U * a * star U) ''
          (quasiLocalNet s₀ O : Set (ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀)) =
        quasiLocalNet s₀ ({y | g⁻¹ • y ∈ O} : Set Λ) := sorry

/-- Independence of the reference configuration: for any two reference configurations $s_0$ and
$s_0'$ (which may differ at infinitely many sites) there is a $*$-isomorphism
$\varphi : \mathcal A_{ql} \to \mathcal A_{ql}'$ of the quasi-local algebras with
$a \in \mathcal A(O) \iff \varphi(a) \in \mathcal A'(O)$ for every set of sites $O$ and every
$a \in \mathcal A_{ql}$, and with $\varphi(\iota_X(m)) = \iota'_X(m)$ for every finite set of
sites $X$ and matrix $m$ on its letters. -/
theorem exists_starAlgEquiv_of_ref {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ s₀' : (u : Λ) → σ u) :
    ∃ φ : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀ ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ] quasiLocalAlgebra s₀',
      (∀ (O : Set Λ) (a : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀),
        (a : ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀) ∈ quasiLocalNet s₀ O ↔
          (φ a : ConfigSpace s₀' →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀') ∈ quasiLocalNet s₀' O) ∧
      ∀ (X : Set Λ) [Fintype X] (m : Matrix ((x : X) → σ x) ((x : X) → σ x) ℂ)
        (a : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀), (a : ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀) = localEmbed s₀ X m →
          (φ a : ConfigSpace s₀' →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀') = localEmbed s₀' X m := sorry

/-! ## The universal property -/

open Classical in
/-- Universal property: let $D$ be a C$^*$-algebra and $(\beta_X)$ a family, indexed by the
finite sets of sites $X$, of unital $*$-homomorphisms from the matrices on the letters of $X$ to
$D$, compatible in the sense $\beta_Y(m \otimes 1) = \beta_X(m)$ for $X \subseteq Y$ (with
$m \otimes 1$ as in the compatibility of the local embeddings). Then there is exactly one unital
$*$-homomorphism $\Phi : \mathcal A_{ql} \to D$ with $\Phi(\iota_X(m)) = \beta_X(m)$ for all $X$
and $m$. -/
theorem exists_unique_lift {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {D : Type*} [CStarAlgebra D]
    (β : ∀ X : Finset Λ,
      Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ →⋆ₐ[ℂ] D)
    (hβ : ∀ (X Y : Finset Λ) (hXY : X ⊆ Y)
      (m : Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ),
      β Y (Matrix.of fun ζ ζ' : (y : (Y : Set Λ)) → σ y =>
        if ∀ y : (Y : Set Λ), (y : Λ) ∉ X → ζ y = ζ' y then
          m (fun x => ζ ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) (fun x => ζ' ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) else 0) = β X m) :
    ∃! Φ : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀ →⋆ₐ[ℂ] D, ∀ (X : Finset Λ)
      (m : Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ)
      (a : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀),
      (a : ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀) = localEmbed s₀ (X : Set Λ) m → Φ a = β X m := sorry

open Classical in
/-- Let $D$ be a C$^*$-algebra other than the zero algebra and $(\beta_X)$ a compatible family as
in the universal property. Then the $*$-homomorphism $\Phi : \mathcal A_{ql} \to D$ with
$\Phi(\iota_X(m)) = \beta_X(m)$ for all $X$ and $m$ is an isometry. -/
theorem isometry_lift {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ] [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)]
    [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) {D : Type*} [CStarAlgebra D] [Nontrivial D]
    (β : ∀ X : Finset Λ,
      Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ →⋆ₐ[ℂ] D)
    (hβ : ∀ (X Y : Finset Λ) (hXY : X ⊆ Y)
      (m : Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ),
      β Y (Matrix.of fun ζ ζ' : (y : (Y : Set Λ)) → σ y =>
        if ∀ y : (Y : Set Λ), (y : Λ) ∉ X → ζ y = ζ' y then
          m (fun x => ζ ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) (fun x => ζ' ⟨x, hXY x.2⟩) else 0) = β X m)
    (Φ : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀ →⋆ₐ[ℂ] D)
    (hΦ : ∀ (X : Finset Λ)
      (m : Matrix ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ((x : (X : Set Λ)) → σ x) ℂ)
      (a : quasiLocalAlgebra s₀),
      (a : ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀) = localEmbed s₀ (X : Set Λ) m → Φ a = β X m) :
    Isometry Φ := sorry

/-! ## Finite volume -/

/-- If the set of sites $\Lambda$ is finite, the quasi-local algebra $\mathcal A_{ql}$ is the
algebra of all bounded operators on $H$. -/
theorem quasiLocalAlgebra_eq_top_of_finite {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) [Finite Λ] :
    quasiLocalAlgebra s₀ = ⊤ := sorry

/-- For a finite set of sites $O$, the algebra $\mathcal A(O)$ is the range of the local
embedding $\iota_O$. -/
theorem quasiLocalNet_eq_range_of_finite {Λ : Type*} {σ : Λ → Type*} [DecidableEq Λ]
    [∀ u, Fintype (σ u)] [∀ u, DecidableEq (σ u)] (s₀ : (u : Λ) → σ u) (O : Set Λ) [Fintype O] :
    (quasiLocalNet s₀ O : Set (ConfigSpace s₀ →L[ℂ] ConfigSpace s₀)) =
      Set.range (localEmbed s₀ O) := sorry

/-! ## Examples

These examples show that the hypotheses of the main theorems can be met and that the objects are
not degenerate. -/

/-- For the chain $\mathbb N$ with two letters per site and the reference configuration $0$, the
quasi-local algebra is not the zero algebra. -/
theorem witness_nontrivial_quasiLocalAlgebra :
    Nontrivial (quasiLocalAlgebra (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2))) := sorry

/-- For the chain $\mathbb N$ with two letters per site and the reference configuration $0$, the
set of finitely supported configurations, which indexes the basis of $H$, is infinite. -/
theorem witness_infinite_finConfig :
    Infinite (FinConfig (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2))) := sorry

/-- For the chain $\mathbb N$ with two letters per site and the reference configuration $0$, the
algebra $\mathcal A(\{0\})$ of the site $0$ contains an element that is not a multiple of the
identity. -/
theorem witness_quasiLocalNet_singleton_not_scalar :
    ∃ a ∈ quasiLocalNet (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2)) {0}, ∀ c : ℂ, a ≠ c • 1 := sorry

/-- For the chain $\mathbb Z$ with two letters per site and the reference configuration $0$,
which is invariant under translations, there is a unitary $U$ on $H$ with
$U \mathcal A(\{0\}) U^* = \mathcal A(\{1\})$. -/
theorem witness_translation_covariant :
    ∃ U ∈ unitary (ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2)) →L[ℂ]
        ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2))),
      (fun a => U * a * star U) '' (quasiLocalNet (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2)) {0} :
        Set (ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2)) →L[ℂ]
          ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2)))) =
        quasiLocalNet (fun _ : ℤ => (0 : Fin 2)) {1} := sorry

/-- For the chain $\mathbb N$ with two letters per site, the reference configurations $0$ and $1$
differ at infinitely many sites, and there is a $*$-isomorphism $\varphi$ of their quasi-local
algebras with $a \in \mathcal A(O) \iff \varphi(a) \in \mathcal A'(O)$ for every set of sites
$O$. -/
theorem witness_references_differing_everywhere :
    {u : ℕ | (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2)) u ≠ (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : Fin 2)) u}.Infinite ∧
      ∃ φ : quasiLocalAlgebra (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2)) ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ]
          quasiLocalAlgebra (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : Fin 2)),
        ∀ (O : Set ℕ) (a : quasiLocalAlgebra (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2))),
          (a : ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2)) →L[ℂ]
              ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2))) ∈
              quasiLocalNet (fun _ : ℕ => (0 : Fin 2)) O ↔
            (φ a : ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : Fin 2)) →L[ℂ]
              ConfigSpace (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : Fin 2))) ∈
              quasiLocalNet (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : Fin 2)) O := sorry

open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator in
/-- For two sites $0, 1$ with two letters each, there is a monotone family $O \mapsto G(O)$, over
the sets of sites, of subsets of the qudit algebra such that every $G(O)$ is closed under the
adjoint, every element of $G(O_1)$ commutes with every element of $G(O_2)$ whenever $O_1$ and
$O_2$ are orthogonal for the region index of the sets of sites, the norm closure of the star
subalgebra generated by the union of all $G(O)$ is the whole qudit algebra, and $G(\{0\})$
contains two elements that do not commute. -/
theorem witness_generated_net_hypotheses :
    ∃ G : Set (Fin 2) →o Set (Matrix (Fin 2 → Fin 2) (Fin 2 → Fin 2) ℂ),
      (∀ O, ∀ a ∈ G O, star a ∈ G O) ∧
      (∀ O₁ O₂, (subsetIndex (Fin 2)).Orthogonal O₁ O₂ → ∀ a ∈ G O₁, ∀ b ∈ G O₂, Commute a b) ∧
      (StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (⋃ O, G O)).topologicalClosure = ⊤ ∧
      ∃ a ∈ G {0}, ∃ b ∈ G {0}, ¬ Commute a b := sorry

/-- For two sites $0$ and $1$ carrying $2$ and $3$ letters, each of the algebras
$\mathcal A(\{0\})$ and $\mathcal A(\{1\})$ of the qudit net contains two elements that do not
commute. -/
theorem witness_qudit_net_noncommutative :
    (∃ a ∈ quditNet (fun u : Fin 2 => Fin ((u : ℕ) + 2)) {0},
      ∃ b ∈ quditNet (fun u : Fin 2 => Fin ((u : ℕ) + 2)) {0}, ¬ Commute a b) ∧
    ∃ a ∈ quditNet (fun u : Fin 2 => Fin ((u : ℕ) + 2)) {1},
      ∃ b ∈ quditNet (fun u : Fin 2 => Fin ((u : ℕ) + 2)) {1}, ¬ Commute a b := sorry

end Sealed
